 performance thinking of the  ankisi piece  on the train right  that instead of thinking of the  performance  as the work itself  that the performance becomes  either a way of charging the work this  is a word that you've used  or  the other  the other way that i'm curious about is  where you're making a piece and then a  performance follows it like for instance  the mandala  or the the girl walking across  that isn't  that would have been a surprise although  i'm sure you had it in your head but it  wasn't planned out right  so  the difference that i'm trying to  distinguish here is between  when you're intentionally making a  decision about a performance and when a  performance inherently happens to the  work itself where it gains its own life  and how much meaning does that have for  you  i  my answer to that is probably  influenced by one of my mentors  the late terry atkins  and it's  basic jazz fundamental and improvisation  fundamental  is that  i'm actually just creating instances  something's always going to happen  and  i don't know what to anticipate but i  anticipate that thing will happen  and  i  it's very hard to put this in words but  what i'm really doing is just setting up  the opportunity for the unknown to take  place  i think that is a very performative  thing and i think it's a vulnerable  thing but i think it's also  a um  an ecstatic  affair because you sort of know  something's going to happen i don't know  what it's gonna be this time i can't  wait for that thing to happen and then  it didn't have it inevitably does happen  the girl running across happened and it  was that  gas from the crowd i was like wow that  would be better than an hour long  performance you know just that six  second instance  um and i can't think of any  work of mine or  proper installation context where  something unexpected like that has  happened something has not happened  okay so uh i have another question here  so given the autobiographical nature of  your work  would you ever return to tagging  uh and they're continuing here to a  question about engaging with an audience  outside of a gallery or rockefeller  center uh signed graffiti kid from  jersey  great question um  yeah i would consider tagging again  there's a new way  not tagging in the tr  the classical sense of tagging  um  but  i think  there's an opportunity for a new type of  tagging and  um my friend from new jersey probably  has some ideas but considering the space  the cyberspace the where the world we  live in right now and our means to  um  distributing information and ideas  there's all kinds there's so many  different forms of tagging  a performative tag usually when moon  medicine does something related to an  exhibition or in a an official  institution or venue we usually go to a  very unofficial  late night bootleg spot and do an  improvised set  very much before after after  that's a tag in my way in a thought  because there's people who go to that  that would never have come to the first  part and vice versa there's some that  might go to both but very  the majority of the crowd at this spot  in the juke joint is very different than  the one that was over here at the ivory  tower  and i commend those who traverse between  but  i think that's a form of tagging yeah  public work is a tag always and then it  gets tagged on so you get you have to  deal with all that but  that that idea that you would start to  span different audiences that you're  you're shifting and moving  um  feels very open source to go back to  that context  okay so the next question is uh  would you say that pain  or violence play a large role in your  works  or perhaps might you categorize the  feelings and themes involved differently  i would categorize them differently i  think pain has influenced  memory history and in some degrees  reality so i would not ignore pain  but i don't think my work comes from a  place of fear and  sadness in fact i think it comes from a  place of  ecstasy  and critique sometimes  but  amongst that you know i think they're  myriad  feelings or or  ideas in the work pain is just one  violence happens  there's bodily violence but i think  there's artistic violence too and i'm a  big fan of that we're talking about  graffiti there's people who think  graffiti is violent whereas others find  it beautiful  taking a found quilt  and making marks on it is sacrilege to  some people am i defacing it or am i  embellishing it i actually really sort  of like straddling the line of all of  those things and not fitting into one of  them  i think that's another thing that's very  important right now um  particularly this is a really niche art  conversation there has been a huge  proliferation of artwork that's been  championed by african americans and  african and you know creators of color  um in general um people say it's  trending they're saying all these  various things but amongst that  the real thing i think you should look  for is tone  and the variety of tone you're seeing in  the work that people are  pushing towards you to say this is hot  and this is hot this is hot and you know  you don't want to i'm interested in  things that have tones that are  unfamiliar  i think a great artwork can make you  cringe if it can do that it's probably a  great artwork if it just makes you smile  and oh you know swipe left you know  whatever  well  that leads perfectly i've got to say  also thank you for the handwriting that  i'm receiving tonight it's very helpful  uh that's that's not sarcasm  they're  legible so this one has to do with  patina  a lot of your work acquires patina scuff  marks tracks bullet holes  tears rips  how do you see the transition to marble  and bronze in this context of objects  that transform over time  well i think the marble does show the  the trace of the hand if you get close  to the pieces they look super smooth but  you get close and you start to see  different  blemishes and things that i actually  sort of like because they add realism to  it  um and i don't want to uh spoil work  that might be happening in the future  but i was influenced by ruins and i've  yet to make ruins so that's a  possibility right um i  tend to construct a bit of a language or  a vocabulary only to deconstruct it  um so you might see deconstructions of  various types coming  all right  okay so i think we'll take one last  question from the audience before  treating you to a slightly cooler room  when constructing the quilts  have you ever had the inclination to  engage in a dialogue with your  grandfather's murals  um my grandfather's murals i'm assuming  john biggers  is that  who  grandfather's work under that assumption  since these are anonymous questions so  um john was actually a cousin  but if that's what we're talking about  um  i what i didn't show and you know what i  did a very large installation at mass  moca  in 2011  and i showed a quilt painting in the  upper mezzanine and i did a full-scale  reproduction of one of john bigger's  murals though it's called the quilting  party and john biger's work was a  mixture of figurative work and  sacred geometrical work figurative  geometry all this you know figurative  abstraction all this stuff was happening  in his murals  and i actually think his work influenced  me to do anything related to patterns in  geometry in the first place that  includes the breakdance floors and all  of that the mandalas i think that was  coming from the tradition set by john  biggers of using geometrical forms in  his artwork i wanted to find a way of  incorporating some of that language and  then putting my own spin on it no pun  intended with breakdancers but um  and then you know obviously that's gone  into the quilts and so on so they're  totally related and i'm glad somebody  asked that question because you guys are  really paying attention that's good okay  great  well thank you sanford i'm gonna pass  the mic to nicholas here i think we can  also in the meantime give a very warm  round of applause and thank you  [Applause]  [Music]  all right  so it falls to me to um uh conclude our  lecture tonight in the most important  way with thanks first and foremost to  sanford for an amazing  mind-blowing beginning to our journey  together for this year  i also want to thank if anyone's left  out there i want to thank our  architecture students  council or asc for the online for the  outdoor jumber tron they set up at their  picnic to allow  all of us to enjoy this lecture together  whether inside or outside  and then i also wanted to  give particular thanks in the context of  inside and outside  to the support and co-sponsorship of  this lecture by  the art culture and technology program  or art program that's lodged here within  our department  i think this  is part of our excitement and our entire  justification for having sanford here  this year is the very special story at  the mit department of architecture of a  constant dialogue between those inside  and outside architecture  and different kinds of creative realms  of course from kyogre keppes to the  spatial architectures of joan jonas to  our own azra akshamija the director of  the art culture and technology program  and artist and architect both here with  us tonight  it's part of  the special work we do here at mit and  the work that sanford will be  instrumental in shaping and influencing  this year  so with that i invite you to join us  again  next week for vernell noel  in a lecture uh hosted by the  computation group in our public lecture  series  titled situation computations craft and  technology and the title alone again  should tell us something about who we  are and what we do here so  thank you again sanford thank you again  audience here thank you audience outside  and audience online whose questions were  also part of the dialogue here tonight  good night and see you next week
 

